// Book One, Chapter 11 (session 12). The days after the fire, then the covens of Rome in the old quarry on the Esquiline, and the decision about the reckoning.
// Two day actions of four, dusk, the council, the hunt chosen. Ends with rest().
@chapter b1c11
@title The Old Quarry
@book burning-city
@version 2

# start
## The third day
$ do phase("day", 2)
$ b1.tiro_keep = "kept"
$ b1.refuge = "none"
$ b1.rufus_told = "unmet"
$ unflag b1.rebuilt
$ unflag b1.felt_below
$ b1.council_voice = "limits"
$ b1.war_terms = "limited"
$ b1.theron_fear = "ignored"
$ b1.cell = "alone"
$ _ruling = ""
$ do discover("the_old_quarry")
The fire burns for the better part of a week, and then it does not so much go out as lose interest. By the third day there is no flame left to speak of, only a long low smoulder in the cellars of the Aventine and a pall over the whole city the colour of an old bruise. The ash falls like grey snow. It lies in the gutters of the Forum and on the steps of the temples and on the backs of the people who walk about in it with their mouths covered, looking for the places where their houses were.
?if b1.fire_toll == "light"
  It is not as bad as it might have been. You hear the sentence said in the street, in the tone of people who are trying to believe it, and you know the exact number of the nights and the stairs that it cost to make it true.
?elif b1.fire_toll == "heavy"
  It is bad. It is the kind of bad that a city does not walk away from, and does not stop talking about, and that will be, for the rest of the lives of the people in it, a date.
?else
  It is as bad as a thing can be and still be a city afterward. They are still bringing them out of the Subura in carts. The carters have stopped speaking to each other. You have walked past them twice, and both times you have found that you could not do the sum.
?end
You have not slept, in the way that you sleep. You have lain down at dawn in the cellar of the house with the others and looked at the dark, and at dusk you have got up again. The sun does not warm you. It has not warmed you since the night, and you have stopped expecting it to.
?if b1.tiro_end == "avenged"
  Nobody in the house has said a word to you about the roof. They know. It is in the way that the carters step aside from you on the stair, and the way that Hanno, passing you with a ledger, inclines his head half an inch lower than before. It is not a reproach. It is the care that people take with a thing that has been made sharp.
?elif b1.tiro_end == "carried"
  The house knows that you ran with him to the river. It was seen by a hundred people, and one of them was a boy who works the wharf for the house, and by the second dawn it was the only thing anyone could tell you. They tell it very kindly. You wish that they would not.
?else
  The house knows that you stayed with him. It was Hanno who found you, afterward, in the street, with the cloak still in your hands, and he did not say anything. He took one end of it, and the two of you folded it, between you, as one folds a flag.
?end
There is a runner's wax from Prisca on the table in your room, and it says what the other one said. The covens of Rome are to meet tonight, at moonrise, in the old quarry on the Esquiline. The house would like to know what you think.
It is a long time until moonrise. The city, in its ruin, still has a good many things to say to you.
