# tiro_room
## Tiro's room
It is the smallest room in the house, under the stair, and it is exactly as untidy as a man who has been dead for three days would have left it, if he had had no idea. There is a cot that has not been slept in for the whole of the night's hours, because he did not sleep at the proper times either. There is a clay cup with a chip out of the rim. There is a wax tablet with a bad drawing on it, scratched with a stylus and then smoothed, and scratched again, of Prisca as a heron.
There is a ledger, the house's plainest, bound in cheap boards. It is open on the table to a page of sums, and it is all in his round hand, and it is wrong. It is wrong in a number of ways that you can follow, step by step, like a man who has gone out of a door and into a wall and then into another door. At the bottom of each page, in the margin, in smaller writing, are the things that he meant to say to someone. *Ask about the cat on the Via Lata.* *Do not tell Hanno that I know what is in the third cask.* *Tell him: the first month is the worst, and then it is only strange.*
There is no name after that last one. There does not have to be.
Hanno is at the door. You did not hear him come, and you never do. He does not come in. He is carrying a lamp, held a long way off, in a stiff old arm, as a man holds a thing that he has had to learn to be careful of.
?if b1.tiro_end == "held"
  "I am told," he says, in the slow old Punic accent that has outlasted three languages, "that he did not die alone."
?elif b1.tiro_end == "carried"
  "I am told," he says, "that he was alive when he reached the water."
?else
  "I am told," he says, "what was done on the roof afterward. I do not ask. I only say that he would not have wished it, and that I know it was not done for him."
?end
He looks at the ledger.
"It is the house's custom," he says, "that what a person leaves is burned with the lamps on the seventh night. I do not think it is a good custom. It has never been asked of me to say so." He looks at you, with his black deep old eyes. "You may have it, if you like. It is the only thing in the building that he made."
:the_ledger
* Take it
  $ b1.tiro_keep = "kept"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ bond.prisca += 1
  You put it under your arm. It weighs nothing. Hanno nods, once, very slowly, and for a moment his old face does something that is not a smile and has the same shape. "Do not try to add it up," he says. "I have tried. It cannot be done." He goes away along the passage with his lamp, and you sit down on the dead man's cot, for the length of time that it takes to read all of the margins, and then you read them again.
* Let the house burn it with the rest, on the seventh night
  $ b1.tiro_keep = "burned"
  $ defiance -= 1
  "It is the custom," says Hanno. He bows his head. "It will be done properly." You find that you were wrong about what you wanted. You wanted someone to tell you that you did not have to carry it. It is a relief, and a loss, and they are the same, and you do not take anything but the chipped cup. It is the only thing that you can bear to touch. You put it in your tunic, where it knocks against your ribs, and you walk out of the house into the ash.
