# edict
## The road the soldiers are using
The edict is up by noon, on the posts of the Forum and the doors of the baths, in clear, flat, official Latin, and by afternoon it has been read aloud at every crossroads by men who were paid to do it. The fire was lit by a sect. It is a sect from the East, from Judea, a sect of people who hate mankind and meet at night and eat a thing that they call a body. Anyone who is a follower of it is to be taken up and brought before the Prefect. It is not stated what will happen after that. It does not need to be.
You have heard the words before. You heard them from Galerius, on the Palatine stair, in the red light, in a gentle dry voice, as a thing that he had been told to say.
The soldiers are using the road down from the Subura to the Tiber, because it is wide, and has been cleared. They are taking people along it in twos and threes, with their hands bound, in the dust, and the neighbours stand at their doors in their ash covered tunics and watch them go by, and some of them are crying and most are not. You see an old woman spit at a man with a bleeding mouth. You see a girl of ten hold a baby up to see.
?if b1.fire_toll == "terrible"
  It is not a hard thing to see why. They have lost the whole of their lives in a night, and here is something to put the blame on, and it has a face.
?end
You are in the lane behind Naevia's shop when it comes to you. It is her. She has come out of her doorway in her scorched apron with a broom, and she takes you by the sleeve. She has not wept, and she does not now.
"The cobbler," she says. "Onesimus. The Greek, at the end of the lane. He is one of them. He is not one of anything, he mends my shoes, he has two girls and a wife who coughs." She is whispering fiercely. "They meet in a loft on the first day of the week and they say something about love, I have heard it through the wall, it is the dullest thing in the world. He did not light anything. He could not light a lamp. The Prefect's men will be at his door by dusk, because someone on this lane has told them, and I know who, and it is a man who owed him money." She lets go. "I do not know why I am telling you. Something about the way that you looked at those men in the fire."
You go down the lane with her. The cobbler's shop is as small as a cupboard and quite undamaged, and it smells of leather and of a kind of stew that is made of nothing at all. He is a short grey man with a very straight back. His wife is on a stool, wrapped in a shawl, and his two girls, fifteen and eleven, are sitting on the floor with their arms round each other and their eyes on the door.
[judean] He greets you in Aramaic, a poor, careful, labouring Aramaic, a Greek's idea of a Galilean's. It is a courtesy. You have not heard it spoken to you in a Roman street for a very long time.
[not judean] He greets you in good Latin, with a slight Greek lilt, and a bow. He is entirely calm. It is the calm of a man who has already settled it with someone else.
"It is very kind," he says, "but you must not." He says it quite gently. "You do not know what you would be doing."
You know exactly what you would be doing.
:the_cobbler
