  It is not clever, and it is not safe, and it is what you do. You wait until the dusk comes on, and the lane is quiet, and you take them through the burned back ways, one by one, with their shawls over their heads and the small girl's hand in yours, down to the cellar grate and the long cool dark of the Emporium. The house sees them come. It says nothing. Hanno brings a lamp, and then he seems to think better of the lamp, and brings bread instead. The younger girl looks at you over the rim of a cup, with her eyes very wide, and says, in a perfectly clear voice, that you are very cold. Her mother hushes her. You tell her that it is the cellar, and she nods, with the grave good sense of the very young, and does not believe it at all.
* Pay for a berth, and put them on a ship at Ostia [once]
  $ _mp_n += 1
  ?if wealth >= 10
    $ b1.refuge = "shipped"
    $ do earn(-10)
    $ humanity += 2
    $ standing += 1
    It costs a good deal of silver, and a bribe to a harbour clerk, and the use of a name that you had hoped to keep out of Ostia, and it costs no secrets. They go in the dark, in the hold of a grain ship bound for Alexandria, with a bundle of tools and a loaf and the cobbler's last pair of boots. He looks back from the gangplank. He has the same straight back. He says, in a low voice, a thing in Greek, that you do not understand, and his wife says, in Latin, "He says that he will pray for you." You tell her that it is not necessary. She says that she knows. It is only that it is the one thing that they have to give.
  ?else
    You do not have the silver. It is a plain, dull, shameful thing, and it is a thing that has not happened to you in a month, and you stand in the cobbler's shop with your purse in your hand and your face getting slowly hot, and you cannot buy a berth on a grain ship for a family of four. You take them, in the end, to the cellars, because it is the only place that you have. It is not what you meant.
