# rufus_talk
## The Aventine station
The station of the Aventine has lost its roof. It has not lost its men. They are sitting about in the yard in the ash, a hundred of them, grey to the eyebrows, eating bread from a basket, with their hooks and buckets laid out in rows, like soldiers after a battle who have not been told that it is over. Rufus is on an upturned bucket by the wall with a scrap of papyrus on his knee, writing very slowly with a reed. He looks up as you come in. He does not smile.
He has a number of very small, very neat lines. He has been writing them for three nights. He has not yet looked at what he has written.
?if b1.rufus_saw == "mercy"
  "I want to ask you a thing," he says, "and I want you to know before I ask it that I have been telling myself for three days that I will not mind the answer."
?elif b1.rufus_saw == "slaughter"
  "I want to ask you a thing," he says, "and I want you to know that I have a hook in my hand, and that I do not at all expect to use it."
?elif b1.rufus_saw == "both"
  "I want to ask you a thing," he says, "and I want you to know that I have two sets of lines in this book, and that I do not know yet which of them is the truth."
?else
  "I want to ask you a thing," he says, "and I want you to know that I have told everyone that I remember nothing, and that it is the only lie that I have ever told that did not feel like one."
?end
He turns the papyrus over.
"There are six men dead in the Velabrum," he says. "They were found this morning in a lamp seller's yard. They had their throats opened. They did not have much blood left in them, and what there was had a grey look. They were grey cloaks. They were the men that I have been looking for since the spring." He draws a long breath. "I did not do it. The Prefect did not do it. I do not think that a man did it. And I want to know what you are."
It is as simple as that. It is as simple as a question put by a plain, tired man on a bucket in the ash.
:the_answer
* Tell him the truth
  $ b1.rufus_told = "told"
  $ velati_regard += 3
  $ secrecy -= 3
  You tell him. You have not told anyone, not in this way, not a mortal, not once, and you find that it is a very short thing to say. You tell him what you are, and what the night is, and that you do not burn, and that it is not a thing that he should write down. He listens as he listens to a fire. When you have finished he is quite still for a long time, and then he says, "Ah," in the voice of a man who has been told a sum that he had already done. He takes up the reed. He puts it down. "I will have to write it," he says. "I am sorry. I think it is the only thing that I am good for." You say that you know. He says, very low, "Thank you for not lying." It is a most peculiar thing to be thanked for.
* Tell him that he saw a man in a great fire, and that it is enough
  $ b1.rufus_told = "lied"
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  $ secrecy += 1
  "I am a man," you say. "I was there, and I was frightened. So were you." It is a smooth, good, usable lie, and he looks at you for a very long time, with his tired, kind, merciless eyes, and does not say that it is one. He nods. He writes something down, and it is very short. You do not need to read it to know what it says. It is not a good thing to have done to a man who is the nearest thing that you have to a friend in the daylight.
* Ask him what he would do with the answer
  $ b1.rufus_told = "deflected"
  $ velati_regard += 1
  "That is a very good question," says Rufus, after a moment. "It is the one that I have been asking myself." He rubs the back of his neck. "I would write it down. And then I would try to be somebody who could be trusted with it." He gives a short dry breath, almost a laugh. "I do not think that I am, yet. I think I am only a man who has seen a good deal and has a roll of paper." He does not press you. It is the kindest thing that he could have done, and it is not an answer, and you both know it.
He puts the papyrus away inside his tunic.
"There is something else," he says. "The Prefect's men have been through the cells at the Esquiline gate. They have been taking out the ones who were taken in the fire, the grey cloaks, the real ones, and I do not know where they are going. I have two of mine who say they have heard nothing. I have one who says that the Prefect has been told to be quiet about them." He looks at the ash on his knuckles. "I should like to know why a man who set the city alight should be quiet."
It is a good question. You are going to carry it for some time.
