# firm
## The counting house
The counting house of Collis has not burned. It is a modest set of rooms over a rope seller's shop near the Forum Boarium, on a street that the wind happened to skip, and it is full of the smell of wet ash and cold tallow. Pollio, who is your clerk, and who is the first man that you ever hired without any sense of what you were doing, is at the long table with a pile of tablets, in a tunic that he has not changed for three days, with his eyes red. He gets up as you come in. He is a man who does not know what to do with his hands, and he puts them behind his back.
"It is a bad week," he says. "It is a very odd week. I have sold more oil than in the last two months." He does not look at you. "I have sold more rope. I have sold lamp wicks that I had in the cellar since the spring, to a man who did not want to be seen to buy them. Everything that a city needs, when it has burned, is a thing that a firm like this has got in a cellar. I should like it to be known that I do not like it."
?if b1.collis_start == "insulae" or b1.collis_start == "lending"
  You have more of the burned city on your books than you would wish. It sits on the long table in the shape of a heap of wax tablets, each with a name and a debt and a roof that is not there. A great many of them will not be paid. A few of them are paid already, in advance, by people who are paying for the privilege of a name that does not know them.
?elif b1.collis_start == "shipping"
  The ships are all late. Every one of them is carrying something that the city wants more than it did a week ago, and the factors in Ostia have raised the price by a third, and by the end of the month it will be a half.
?elif b1.collis_start == "gladiators" or b1.collis_start == "slaves"
  The school has been taking in the people who have been put out of their homes, at a fee that is called a contract, and the ledger is very careful about the wording. You read it for a long time. It is entirely legal. Pollio, beside you, looks at his boots.
?else
  The wine came through unburned. It is a tidy, sordid little miracle, and it is the only part of the books that does not make you feel unwell.
?end
"You will want to know what to do with it," says Pollio. "It is a great deal of money, in a week, and it is not honest, though it is not dishonest. It is only that we have the right things."
There is a bad, practical, human idea that sits on the table between you. A firm of this size, in a burned city, has it in its power to put a roof on a street.
:the_purse
