# dusk
## Dusk
$ do phase("night", 1)
The day goes out in a long copper smear behind the smoke. You go up through the Esquiline in the grey light, in a road that is full of people going the other way, with bundles, and a handcart, and a donkey with a bird cage tied to its pack, and they do not look at you.
?if must_feed()
  Hunger comes up in you on the road like water in a cellar. You cannot go into the quarry with it. You turn aside, to the lane behind a butcher's, where the house keeps an arrangement for such things, and it is done quickly, and quietly, and without a word.
  ?if b1.vow == "animal"
    $ do feed("animal")
  ?else
    $ do feed("donor")
  ?end
?end
Hanno comes with you. He has a stick, and a cloak with the hood up, and he does not talk. Prisca is already ahead of you. She has gone up alone, before the sun was down, in her plain brown cloak and her good shoes, with her hands folded, as she goes up to every difficult room in Rome, and you are the last of the house to follow.
?if b1.coven == "stayed"
  "You will not be asked to speak for the house," says Hanno. It is the longest thing that he has said to you in a month. "You will be asked to speak for yourself. I should like you to know that it is the same thing, now." He walks on a few paces. "It was so for the boy."
  $ bond.hanno += 1
?else
  "You are not of the house," says Hanno, "and you are going to the quarry as the house's friend. It is a very particular thing to be. I should think carefully about what it is that a friend owes." He does not say more. He seems to feel that he has said a good deal.
?end
The old quarry is on the far side of the hill, on land that nobody owns, where the city's builders cut the soft yellow stone for the first walls, and where nobody has dug for four hundred years. It is a long ragged hollow in the hillside with a rim of broken brush, open to the sky, and a mouth in the cliff at the far end, black as a lamp wick, with the dark of the old galleries behind it. There is no light. Nobody has brought a flame. It has not been discussed, and it has not needed to be.
-> quarry

# quarry
## The old quarry
:quarry
The moon is late. It has not yet come over the rim of the hill, and the sky above the quarry is the grey pearl of a sky that has had a fire in it, and in the grey, at the bottom of the hollow, in the broken yellow stone, there are forty people standing in the dark.
They have not drawn together. That is the first thing that you see. They have come from every quarter of the city, from the Palatine and the Egyptian quarter and the river and the Greek temples, from the old houses and the old lines, and they are standing in the quarry in their groups, as people stand at a funeral where there has been a quarrel. Each of them is very still. You can feel them, as you come over the rim, as you feel the heat of a furnace through a wall. It is a hundred and fifty years of living for every ten of them. It is more than that, in some.
The Senate counts five lines in Rome. The five stand in the hollow. You can tell them by their way of standing. The Merchant house, round Prisca, is a close dark huddle like a group of clerks who have been called to the master's office. The Priestess line has come with its veils, and the Physician line with its quiet bags, and they have kept very much to themselves. And at the far end, between two broken walls of yellow stone, with their backs to the cliff, there are two others, and they do not stand together at all.
The first is a knot of very big, very scarred men and women, in the old plain leather of the legions, with their cloaks pinned, and at their head, with a hand resting on a short sword that he does not need, a broad grizzled man with half an ear and a face like a worked field. He is laughing. It is a short, flat, unhappy laugh, and he is looking at the sky. That is the General's line. That is the one that the Senate uses when it needs a thing to be done. He is called Theron, and he is the oldest soldier in Rome.
The second is a small, upright woman in a plain linen robe and a great deal of very old gold, with a long dark face and her arms folded, and she is looking at everything, and she is not looking at anyone. That is the Scribe line. Her name is Meritamun, and she has the air of a woman who is waiting for a lecture to be over so that she may correct it.
There are no Lictors. There is only Galerius. He has come alone, in his grey cloak, with his tablet, and he is standing at the edge of the hollow, a little apart, as a witness stands. He inclines his head to you as you come down the slope.
?if b1.galerius == "hostile"
  It is quite a friendly nod. That is what is so difficult about it.
?elif b1.galerius == "wary"
  It is the nod of a man who has been told that you are dangerous and has not yet decided to believe it.
?else
  It is the nod of an old acquaintance, who has seen a good deal that evening and is a little tired.
?end
$ do discover("general_house")
$ do discover("scribe_house")
$ do meet("theron")
$ do meet("meritamun")
?if b1.coven == "alone"
  Nobody has asked you where you are standing. You go down into the hollow by yourself, and stand alone at the edge of the stone, and it is a long moment, and a man behind you in a Priestess veil says something quiet and dry, and somebody laughs, and then Prisca turns her head and sees you, and stops it with one look.
?else
  You go down into the hollow with the house, and you stand with it. It is a plain thing, and it is a warm one, and it is, you realise, as you take your place at Prisca's elbow, the first time that you have been glad of it since the roof.
?end
Between the walls at the far end, at the very back of the quarry, there is a door. You did not see it at first. It is set into the living cliff, bronze, green with age, taller than two men, with a great ring in the middle of it and a drain at the foot, and it has no handle. The General's line has put itself in front of it, without looking round, as one sits with one's back against a thing in the dark. Theron's left hand, at his side, has come to rest on the bronze. He does not seem to know it.
There are a few minutes before the moon. You have a little time. It is a thing that you can spend.
:the_door
* Go close to the bronze door and listen
